      "anonymous_summary_of_answers": "Both specialists recommend an orthopedic or sports‑medicine clinician as the most appropriate initially. (No alternative specialty was suggested).",
      "extreme_views": []
    },
    {
      "id": "U4",
      "question": "What are the principal alternative diagnoses and how can they be distinguished from MTSS?",
      "anonymous_summary_of_answers": "All experts listed tibial stress fracture, exertional compartment syndrome, and vascular/vascular‑claudication–like entities; Expert B added medial tibial periostitis and highlighted that meniscal pathology is not relevant. Distinguishing features include focal point tenderness or night pain for fracture, neurologic changes for compartment syndrome, and unlike MTSS, a more localized bone line on imaging or neurovascular compromise.",
      "extreme_views": []
    },
    {
      "id": "U5",
      "question": "Are any red‑flag findings present that would change the management to urgent assessment?",
      "anonymous_summary_of_answers": "All experts agreed that the current findings – no rest or night pain, swelling, erythema, neurovascular loss or systemic symptoms – are not red flags.",
      "extreme_views": []
    },
    {
      "id": "U6",
      "question": "What diagnostic studies, if any, are indicated at this stage?",
      "anonymous_summary_of_answers": "Plain images are not routinely needed; they are only advised if symptoms persist, worsen, or warning signs develop. Expert B singled out radiographs for suspected stress fracture; Expert C mentioned MRI or bone scan only for persistent or suspicious cases.",
      "extreme_views": []
    },
    {
      "id": "U7",
      "question": "What first‑line conservative management should be initiated?",
      "anonymous_summary_of_answers": "Consensus includes activity modification, footwear replacement, ice/compression, and targeted flexibility/strengthening (calf, tibialis anterior, hip abductors). Expert B splits the plan into five steps with a gradual return‑to‑run protocol; Expert C adds a formal physical‑therapy biomechanical assessment.",
      "extreme_views": []
    },
    {
      "id": "U8",
      "question": "What return‑to‑activity guidance and warning signs should be communicated to the patient?",
      "anonymous_summary_of_answers": "Both experts stress a gradual, pain‑free progression and advise return if pain recurs at rest/night, becomes localized, or is associated with swelling, numbness, or worsening after 4 weeks of therapy. (Expert BDetails red‑flags for compartment syndrome or fracture).",
      "extreme_views": []
    },
    {
      "id": "U9",
      "question": "What modifiable risk factors/underlying contributors should be evaluated and addressed?",
      "anonymous_summary_of_answers": "All identify rapid mileage increase, hard surfaces, and worn shoes as clear contributors. Experts B and C additionally highlight the female athlete triad, footwear biomechanics (overpronation), and potential anterior tibialis fatigue syndrome.",
      "extreme_views": []
    }
  ],
  "merged_additional_considerations": [
    "Assess menstrual cycle regularity, nutritional intake, and bone health to rule out the female athlete triad.",
    "Perform gait and biomechanical analysis to detect overpronation or other foot‑strike abnormalities.",
    "Educate the patient about the typical time‑frame for symptom improvement (often weeks) and set realistic expectations.",
    "Consider psychosocial stressors (e.g., exam pressure) that may affect pain perception and adherence to rehab."
  ],
  "anonymous_overall_summary": "The panel unanimously diagnosed the patient with medial tibial stress syndrome, an overuse injury that is neither emergent nor hazardous at this time. Orthopedic or sports‑medicine clinicians should guide management, which revolves around activity modification, proper footwear, ice, and structured strengthening/stretching of the lower‑extremity musculature. Imaging is unnecessary unless red‑flag signs develop or symptoms persist beyond four weeks. patients should be counseled on a graded return‑to‑running plan, warned about pain at rest, night pain or neurologic changes, and have modifiable contributors like training errors, surface, shoes, biomechanics, and possible components of the female athlete triad evaluated and addressed."
}

